# Her End
Tamil title: அவள் முடிவு

She had lost both her husband and her child. Yet she had been beautiful. Her name was Amirtham, but fate had dissolved poison into her life.

Amirtham was the beloved of a wealthy man. That wealthy man had an only son, Madhavan, who was as handsome as Lord Shiva. For him, the son of wealth, the wedding was conducted on an auspicious day chosen according to the astrologers' instructions. What pomp! He won the lovely Amirtham as his wife, a fitting match for a young man of beauty and means.

A few months passed. Madhavan left Amirtham, his bride dearer to him than his own eyes, and died.

Two months later, Madhavan was to marry again! Had he been born a woman, would he have possessed the right to remarry? In this Hindu religion, a woman who has lost her husband has no right to remarry! Instead, she must accept restraint and widowhood. Had her brother not been married under precisely that arrangement? In the wedding gathering, she saw everyone except such "ill omens" as herself delighting in the company of their husbands. Her heart was deeply wounded. She grieved that she had been born into this accursed Hindu faith and sank into an ocean of sorrow.

Madhavan's remarriage was celebrated: her brother, who had lost his wife after she had lost her husband, could marry again. But what of her?

Amirtham was not old. She was only about eighteen. She broke through society's restrictions. Yet she never imagined that this very act would bring about her "end."

Some new people had moved into the house opposite hers. The attractiveness of a young man in that house delighted Amirtham's heart. His true nature cannot be left unsaid: he was lustful and base. How could she have known? She was captivated by his beauty.

He cast his net for that young woman whose longing had no legitimate outlet. She was caught on the hook. Was she not human too? Did she not possess the feelings of youth?

They met. Before long, the matter had spread through every lane and corner of the town. In furtive whispers people said that Amirtham had brought disgrace upon the honour of her father, Somu Mudaliar's family. Their final meeting seems to have come then. Amirtham asked him to run away from the town with her. He went away saying, "I will tell you tomorrow," and that was the last she saw of him.

One day, Amirtham saw the house opposite being vacated. She learned that the young man was travelling to another town for his wedding. Until then she had remained unconcerned by the gossip and had trusted him; now her heart thrashed like a kendai fish caught on a hook.

She had read that men are bees and women are flowers. Even so, she had yielded to him. What does not happen in this world? Do moths that love the radiance of a lamp not approach it and perish in its heat?

"For love, I will face anything! No matter how much opposition there is, I will not fear," he had once said. That helpless woman did not understand that his words were deceit, one lesson from the ancient course by which men have learned to cheat women. He had even explained that the word "widow" carries one meaning in the orthodox lexicon and another in the world of reason. Only after his words and deeds proved contrary could she understand that all of it had been pretence. The realization came too late.

She had built castles in her mind, believing that she might find happiness, but she found only suffering. Society's structure triumphed by destroying the woman who had tried secretly to break its restraints. There, beneath the fig tree, her body swings in the wind. Will our society ever be reformed?

4 November 1945
